---

## Intuition
If we think of the graph as a map, we want to know: **can we travel from the source city to the destination city?** We simply start at the source and explore all reachable nodes level by level (BFS). If we ever land on the destination, a valid path exists. If we exhaust all reachable nodes without finding it, no path exists.

---

## Approach
1. **Build an adjacency list** from the given edge list so we can efficiently look up neighbors of any node.
2. **BFS from source** — push source into a queue and mark it visited.
3. At each step, pop a node. If it's the destination, return `true`.
4. Otherwise, push all unvisited neighbors into the queue and mark them visited (to avoid revisiting).
5. If the queue empties without hitting the destination, return `false`.

---

## Complexity
- **Time Complexity: O(V + E)**
  - Every node (V) and edge (E) is processed at most once.
- **Space Complexity: O(V + E)**
  - Adjacency list takes O(V + E), visit array takes O(V), queue takes O(V) in the worst case.

---

## Code
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool validPath(int n, vector<vector<int>>& adj, int source, int destination) {

        // Step 1: Build adjacency list
        vector<vector<int>> edges(n);
        for (auto& e : adj) {
            edges[e[0]].push_back(e[1]);
            edges[e[1]].push_back(e[0]);
        }

        // Step 2: BFS
        queue<int> q;
        q.push(source);
        vector<int> visit(n, 0);
        visit[source] = 1;

        while (!q.empty()) {
            int node = q.front();
            q.pop();

            // Found destination
            if (node == destination) return true;

            // Explore unvisited neighbors
            for (int i = 0; i < edges[node].size(); i++) {
                if (!visit[edges[node][i]]) {
                    visit[edges[node][i]] = 1;
                    q.push(edges[node][i]);
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```
